The singleton and binary join maps into the powerdomain are approximable, and the binary join is computable.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDsingleton_mono`).** If \(X'\subseteq X\) are neighbourhoods, then the singleton generated by the principal element at \(X\) lies below the singleton generated at \(X'\).

*Proof.* Every neighbourhood containing an upper set witnessed above \(X\) is also witnessed above the smaller neighbourhood \(X'\). ∎

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDsingletonApproxMap`).** The singleton approximable map sends a neighbourhood \(X\) to the singleton powerdomain element generated by the principal element at \(X\).

*Proof.* The preceding antitonicity in neighbourhood refinement gives the required monotonicity of the relation. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDsingletonApproxMap_rel`).** For a neighbourhood \(X\), the singleton map relates \(X\) to \(W\) exactly when \(W\) belongs to the singleton generated by the principal element at \(X\).

*Proof.* This is the relation induced by the monotone assignment. ∎

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDsingletonMeet`).** The meet of two powerdomain elements consists of the neighbourhoods belonging to both.

*Proof.* Common membership is closed under intersection and enlargement and contains the master neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDsingletonMeet_mem`).** A neighbourhood belongs to the singleton meet of \(a\) and \(b\) exactly when it belongs to both \(a\) and \(b\).

*Proof.* This is the defining membership condition. ∎

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDfinJoinApproxMap₂`).** The binary finite-join relation holds when each input is related to the target by the singleton approximable map.

*Proof.* Domain and codomain validity, target intersections, and monotonicity hold componentwise. ∎

**Definition (`NeighborhoodSystem.finJoinMap_prod`).** The product form of the binary finite-join map is obtained by viewing the binary relation as an approximable map out of the product system.

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.finJoinMap_prod_rel`).** On a product neighbourhood generated by \(X,Y\), the product join relation is exactly the binary finite-join relation on \(X,Y\).

*Proof.* The two product preimages are precisely \(X\) and \(Y\). ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDext`).** Two powerdomain elements are equal when they have the same neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* Elements of any neighbourhood system are determined extensionally by membership. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDfinJoinApproxMap₂_rel_iff`).** The binary finite-join relation holds exactly when both singleton relations hold.

*Proof.* This is its defining condition. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDfinJoin_pair`).** The two-term finite join of \(x,y\) is the meet of their singleton powerdomain elements.

*Proof.* Both sides contain exactly those powerdomain neighbourhoods that contain suitable generated upper sets for both \(x\) and \(y\). ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDfinJoin_inter_two`).** A neighbourhood belongs to the two-term finite join exactly when it belongs to each singleton.

*Proof.* Combine the two-term join identity with the membership rule for the singleton meet. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDfinJoinApproxMap₂_rel_PD`).** On neighbourhoods \(X,Y\), the binary relation to \(W\) holds exactly when \(W\) belongs to the two-term join of the corresponding principal elements.

*Proof.* Each conjunct is singleton membership for one principal element, and two-term join membership is their conjunction. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDsingletonApproxMap_rel_Ypd_iff`).** The singleton map relates the neighbourhood indexed by \(n\) to the coded powerdomain neighbourhood \(k\) exactly when some index in the decoded list for \(k\) names a neighbourhood containing the one indexed by \(n\).

*Proof.* Membership of the generator in the coded finite union selects exactly such a list entry. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDsingletonApproxMap_toElementMap`).** The element map induced by the singleton approximable map sends \(x\) to its singleton powerdomain element.

*Proof.* A witness neighbourhood below \(x\) can be enlarged within \(x\), and conversely a singleton witness itself supplies a source neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDfinJoinApproxMap₂_toElementMap`).** The element map induced by the binary finite-join relation sends \(x,y\) to their two-term powerdomain join.

*Proof.* Binary relation membership splits into the two singleton images, whose conjunction is two-term join membership. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDsingleton_principal`).** The singleton of a principal element generated by \(X\) equals the principal powerdomain element generated by the upper set of \(X\).

*Proof.* On both sides, membership means being a powerdomain neighbourhood containing that generated upper set. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.ext_of_principal`).** Two approximable maps are equal if their element maps agree on every principal element.

*Proof.* A relation at \(X\) is equivalent to target membership in the image of the principal element at \(X\). ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.finJoinMap_prod_toElementMap`).** The product join map sends a pair of elements to their two-term powerdomain join.

*Proof.* Convert the product map to its binary form and apply the binary element-map identity. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.singleton_isComputable`).** Relative to the effective powerdomain presentation, the singleton approximable map is computable.

*Proof.* Its indexed relation is the decidable bounded search for a decoded generator containing the source neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`NeighborhoodSystem.PDfinJoinApproxMap₂_isComputable`).** The product form of the binary finite-join map is computable.

*Proof.* Its relation is the conjunction of two computable singleton relations after primitive-recursively selecting the two product coordinates. ∎
